\cf2 \strokec4 Three knobs the literature actually hands you for C10 \'97 and all three sit on the gap your own graph flags (Layer Editing \uc0\u8594  Token Prediction):\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 1. Where the key is read, not how hard you write it.\strokec4  Hernandez (ICLR 2024) and the LRE-faithfulness line say relations decode linearly from the \strokec5 subject's last token\strokec4 . Your port keys at the last \strokec5 prompt\strokec4  token \'97 that's not a ridge problem, it's a key-site problem. Knob: subject-token keying, with the LRE admission test as a pre-registered gate (if the relation isn't linearly decodable, no amount of \uc0\u955  tuning will transfer).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 2. Projection side.\strokec4  Sun et al. (ISSTA 2026) is the sharpest surfaced finding: down-projection edits (MEMIT's and AlphaEdit's target) collapse capability far worse than up-projection edits (38.9 \uc0\u8594  20.5 Pass@1), and sequential edits compound it on 3B models. Your locality breakage (+0.26\'960.35 nats generic NLL, JS 83\'9687) is the predicted signature. Knob: move the write to the up-projection and re-measure locality before touching paraphrase.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 3. Generalization as an objective, not a side-effect.\strokec4  GLIME makes the key move \'97 pair the edit with preference optimization \strokec5 over generation behavior\strokec4 , plus a gradient constraint. Paraphrase 0/72 is exactly overfitting-to-target-prompt, the failure GLIME was built against. TAXI adds the uncomfortable control: even when consistency appears, it's marginal and far below human baseline, and \strokec5 easier on atypical subjects\strokec4  \'97 so pick your paraphrase held-out set knowing typicality is a confound.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The non-obvious connection: knobs 1 and 2 are both \strokec5 siting\strokec4  decisions (which token, which matrix), knob 3 is an \strokec5 objective\strokec4  decision. Your C10 log has been tuning a scalar (\uc0\u955 ) across a space where the live variables are categorical. Multi-token VALUE routing (Route C side-store) only becomes the right question after the LRE test comes back.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec6 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa535\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec2 Elaborating the three knobs\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 The siting/objective split is the real finding\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Your C10 log reads as a one-dimensional search: \uc0\u955  \u8712  \{10, 1, 0.01\}, L2 \u8594  0.01, re-run, re-measure. Amendment 3 restored 24/24 on the trained prompt and moved paraphrase 0/72 \u8594  0/72. That's the signature of a search in the wrong space. \u955  is a \strokec5 magnitude\strokec4  parameter; the three knobs below are \strokec5 categorical\strokec4  \'97 which token, which matrix, which loss. No setting of a magnitude reaches a category.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
