\cf2 \strokec3 Knob 1 \'97 Key site (subject-last-token vs. prompt-last-token)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 The claim.\strokec4  Hernandez et al. (ICLR 2024) show relations decode as approximately linear functions of the hidden state at the \strokec5 subject's\strokec4  final token. Your port keys at the last \strokec5 prompt\strokec4  token. Those coincide only when the subject terminates the prompt \'97 which held for your trained template and broke for every paraphrase.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 Why the numbers confirm it, not just suggest it.\strokec4  Hidden-state transfer 0.8\'961.8% \strokec5 at any ridge\strokec4 . Ridge-invariance is the diagnostic: if the write were correctly sited but under/over-regularised, transfer would move with L2. It didn't. The key is landing on a state that paraphrases don't reconstruct, so there is nothing for \uc0\u955  to trade off against.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 The gate.\strokec4  Run the LRE-faithfulness admission test \strokec5 before\strokec4  any re-implementation. If the target relation isn't linearly decodable at the subject position, subject-keying buys you nothing either \'97 and you've learned that for the cost of a local read. This is the cheap move in your Forward (i), and it should stay first.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 Secondary.\strokec4  Your own note already has the pre-edit NLL-by-position curve as an admission signal. That's the same instrument pointed at a different question \'97 use both; they cross-validate.\
