\cf2 \strokec3 Knob 2 \'97 Projection side (down vs. up)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 The claim.\strokec4  Sun et al. (ISSTA 2026): editing the down-projection collapses capability (Pass@1 38.9 \uc0\u8594  20.5); up-projection edits don't, at comparable edit success. Sequential edits on a 3B compound it (23.1 \u8594  12.8). MEMIT-family solves \'97 including the AlphaEdit-ARE path you ported \'97 target down-proj.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 Why this is your locality result, not a coincidence.\strokec4  Generic NLL +0.26\'960.35 nats, real-country 10/12, JS 83\'9687. That is capability collapse localised to the edited band, measured in a different currency than Sun's Pass@1 but pointing the same direction. You're on a 3B. You're running a sequence of edits. You are inside the exact cell their table is worst in.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 What makes this knob attractive.\strokec4  It is orthogonal to Knob 1. Siting the \strokec5 key\strokec4  (which token) and siting the \strokec5 write\strokec4  (which matrix) are independent decisions, so they can be tested in a 2\'d72 rather than a chain \'97 four runs, not four sequential debugging rounds. And locality is measurable\
