\cf2 \strokec3 The transfer: a three-role write\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Your Route (ii) already has the pieces, but it's framed as \strokec5 more keys\strokec4 . Reframe it as \strokec5 different kinds of keys\strokec4 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 | Role | Video analogue | Edit site | Function |\
|---|---|---|---|\
| \strokec3 Sink\strokec4  | global stability frames | subject-last-token (Hernandez site) | the relation-invariant anchor; survives paraphrase by construction if LRE-faithful |\
| \strokec3 Tail\strokec4  | short-term continuity | last-prompt-token (your current site) | preserves the canonical trained-prompt read \'97 the thing AnyEdit/AnyEdit++ actually score |\
| \strokec3 History\strokec4  | mid-range motion | per-target carrier tokens, multi-token VALUE | mid-range expression; selected to \strokec5 diversify\strokec4 , not to duplicate the Sink |\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Current C10 writes \strokec3 Tail only\strokec4 . It restores 24/24 on the trained prompt and nothing else \'97 precisely the behaviour the Tail role predicts,\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec6 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 The corpus offers three in-weight routes, and they split exactly along the gap the graph exposes \'97 the write-mechanics cluster (edit/subject/keyed/band) never touches the tokenizer cluster (token/tokens/prediction). That gap \strokec5 is\strokec4  the C10 problem: you've been tuning W-realization knobs for a failure whose cause lives upstream, at tokenization.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 1. Shorten the value (tokenizer-side, the unexplored gap).\strokec4 \
GS-Quant is the cleanest bearing: extend the vocabulary so each entity is a \strokec5 fixed 4-token code\strokec4 , train code embeddings + LoRA, base frozen. This collapses the 4\'965.5-token coined continuation that makes full-sequence exact-match compound. STRR is the diagnostic you're missing \'97 it literally measures "proportion of words preserved as single tokens," i.e. a \strokec5 pre-registrable predictor\strokec4  of which project-coined terms will be fragile before you edit. Your robust arms were single-token; STRR turns that observation into a screening metric. The published gap: GS-Quant scores link prediction over candidate sets, never paraphrase read-back \'97 so option (iv)'s falsifier is exactly "does a learned short code answer a natural-language paraphrase?"\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 2. Mark the span instead of minting a token.\strokec4 \
KILM wraps multi-token entities in `<ent>\'85</ent>` delimiters with masked description spans. Cheaper cousin of (1), and its own warning attached: "catastrophic forgetting \'85 unavoidable in the data upscaling setting," plus it needs fine-tuning \'97 outside your engine-untouched constraint. Honest status: bearing, not a route, unless you relax that constraint.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 3. Per-token editing (AnyEdit).\strokec4 \
Still the only mechanism aimed directly at the W-realization bottleneck CORPUS/36 isolated, and the band-knob and 4\'9612 failures only eliminated the cheap alternatives \'97 they raised AnyEdit's priority without proving its necessity. But CORPUS/41 says the cost is a multi-day port with 17 hard CUDA bindings and a struck reduction anchor.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 The asymmetry worth acting on:\strokec4  routes 1 and 3 attack the same quantity \'97 tokens-per-value \'97 but route 3 edits \strokec5 per token\strokec4  while route 1 \strokec5 reduces the token count\strokec4 . Route 1 is strictly cheaper and currently untested. And CORPUS/43's keying result constrains both: if the write is read from the frame rather than the subject, no amount of value-shortening rescues transfer. Key at the subject's last token, then shorten the value.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 Suggested order:\strokec4  STRR screen on your 24 A7 values (hours, no GPU) \uc0\u8594  does token-length predict parafull? If yes, a short-code probe before the AnyEdit port. If no, the bottleneck isn't length and AnyEdit's rationale weakens too.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec6 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa535\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec2 The Asymmetry, Unpacked\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 The statement:\strokec4  routes 1 and 3 attack the same quantity \'97 tokens-per-value \'97 but route 3 edits \strokec5 per token\strokec4  while route 1 \strokec5 reduces the token count\strokec4 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 Why they're the same quantity\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 CORPUS/35's own diagnosis: coined values tokenize at 4\'965.5 tokens, and full-sequence exact-match \strokec3 compounds\strokec4  per-token failure. If each token realizes with probability \strokec5 p\strokec4 , the A7 parafull you measure is roughly \strokec5 p\strokec4 \uc0\u8319 . Your 19\'9631% at n\u8776 5 implies per-token \strokec5 p\strokec4  \uc0\u8776  0.72\'960.79 \'97 not catastrophic per token, catastrophic only under exponentiation. That exponent is the whole C10 residual.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Two ways to attack \strokec5 p\strokec4 \uc0\u8319 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa30\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 AnyEdit (route 3):\strokec5  raise p \uc0\u8594  1 by writing each token position explicitly. Fixes the base.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 Short codes (route 1):\strokec5  drop n from 5 to 4 \'97 or, more aggressively, to 1 if the value is a single minted token. Fixes the exponent.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Same product, opposite factor. Nothing in CORPUS/36\'9638 distinguishes them, because the band knobs and 4\'9612 sweeps touched neither \'97 they tuned \strokec5 where\strokec4  and \strokec5 how wide\strokec4  the write lands, not how long the value is.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 Why route 1 is strictly cheaper\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 | | AnyEdit | Short code |\
|---|---|---|\
| Blocker | 17 CUDA bindings, struck reduction anchor, multi-day port, device-port deviation must be pre-registered | vocabulary extension + embedding rows; MEMIT engine untouched |\
| Anchor status | differential anchor \strokec3 struck\strokec4  (memitARE \uc0\u8800  MEMIT) | static identity anchor intact \'97 same engine, different value encoding |\
| Screening cost | none available pre-port | \strokec3 hours, CPU-only\strokec4  (STRR on 24 A7 values) |\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Route 1 has a \strokec5 free diagnostic in front of it\strokec4 . Route 3 does not. That alone orders them.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 The exponent is also testable without building either\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 STRR screen: compute token-length per A7 value, regress against observed parafull.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa30\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 Monotone fit\strokec5  \uc0\u8594  the wall is length, exactly as CORPUS/35 claims. Route 1 becomes the lead, and route 3's rationale strengthens as a fallback, not as the first spend.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 Flat fit\strokec5  \uc0\u8594  length is a confound. CORPUS/35's "because real coined values tokenize LONG" is a post-hoc story, the bottleneck is prior-coherence per se, and \strokec3 AnyEdit's rationale weakens too\strokec5  \'97 per-token writing doesn't help if the failure isn't per-token.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 That second branch is the one worth naming: the STRR screen can falsify both routes at once. For a multi-day CUDA port with a struck anchor, a few CPU-hours that might kill the motivation is the cheapest insurance in the register.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 The constraint from CORPUS/43 that binds both\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Keying is prior to encoding. If\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec6 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 The constraint from CORPUS/43 that binds both\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Keying is prior to encoding. If the write is read from the \strokec3 frame\strokec4  rather than from the subject, the value's token-length is irrelevant \'97 you can mint a single perfect token and it still won't fire on a natural read, because the retrieval key never points at the subject. CORPUS/43's N=1 frame-keyed arm is the proof: the edit \strokec5 landed\strokec4  (6/6), it just landed attached to the wrong thing \'97 0/18 paraphrases, and it bled onto never-edited fictional frame prompts at 2\'966 of 6. A correct value delivered to a wrong address.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 So the ordering is forced:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa30\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 Key at the subject's last token\strokec5  (K1: A1 95.8 / A2 97.2, singles 18/18 transfer \'97 already established, no new work).\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 Then\strokec5  shorten the value.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Inverted, you'd spend the AnyEdit port or the vocab extension on a system whose read-path is still frame-anchored, and attribute the failure to length.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 What this does to the diagnosis\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 CORPUS/43 also \strokec5 rescues\strokec4  some of C10's residual. The 19\'9631% A7 parafull was measured on batch writes whose keying was never isolated as a variable. Part of that deficit may be keying leakage, not value-length compounding \'97 the two were confounded in every C10 run so far. That's a third branch the STRR screen can't see, and it's cheap to separate: rerun a \strokec5 subject-keyed\strokec4  A7 slice at N=1, single-token value, and read the parafull ceiling. If it's near 95%, keying was clean and length is the whole story. If it's depressed, length is only part of the exponent.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 The sequencing that falls out\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 | Step | Cost | Kills what if negative |\
|---|---|---|\
| STRR regression on 24 A7 values | CPU-hours | both route 1 and route 3 |\
| Subject-keyed N=1 single-token ceiling | one run, existing engine | separates keying from length |\
| Short-code probe (4-token minted value) | vocab rows + LoRA, engine untouched | option (iv) |\
| AnyEdit port | multi-day, 17 CUDA bindings, struck anchor | \'97 only after the above |\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The first two cost almost nothing and gate a multi-day spend with a struck differential anchor. That's the whole argument for running them first.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 The honest residual\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 None of this touches the deeper issue the graph keeps circling \'97 `coin \uc0\u8596  multus` sitting in its own cluster, barely connected to `[[tokens]]`. Project-coined values are fragile \strokec5 because they're novel to the base model\strokec4 , and shortening their encoding is a compression trick, not a prior-installation. GS-Quant gets away with it because link prediction scores over a candidate set; C10 demands natural-language read-back from an open vocabulary. A 4-token code might hold the fact and still not surface when someone asks a paraphrase \'97 which is precisely the gap option (iv)'s falsifier exists to fill, and precisely why the STRR screen is a \strokec5 predictor\strokec4 , not a solution.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec6 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa535\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec2 The Transcending Move: Stop Editing Facts, Edit the Tokenizer's Prior\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Every route on the table \'97 AnyEdit, short codes, KILM, band knobs \'97 accepts one unexamined premise: \strokec3 the value must be written as a sequence the model emits token-by-token under a next-token objective.\strokec4  C10 fires because that objective compounds. But the compounding isn't a property of the value. It's a property of the \strokec5 decoding regime\strokec4 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 The statement\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 > \strokec3 A multi-token value is only fragile because you ask for it autoregressively. Make the value's realization non-autoregressive and the exponent disappears \'97 not reduced, structurally absent.\strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Fast SceneScript is sitting in your corpus as a tokenization paper and you've been reading it as one. It isn't. It's an existence proof that \strokec5 structured\strokec4  outputs can be emitted by multi-token prediction heads with self-speculative verification, converting an n-step serial gamble into a one-step parallel commit plus a cheap accept/reject. The accuracy loss MTP normally incurs \'97 "unreliable token predictions" \'97 is exactly the \strokec5 p\strokec4 \uc0\u8319  problem, and SSD is their fix for it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Your A7 values are structured outputs. Project-coined multi-word terms are closed-form, enumerable, known-at-write-time. You are using an open-vocabulary autoregressive decoder to emit strings you already have in full at edit time.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 Why this leaves the graph\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The three clusters never touch because each assumes the others are fixed. Cluster 2 (edit/keyed/\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec6 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 Why this leaves the graph\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The three clusters never touch because each assumes the others are fixed. Cluster 2 (edit/keyed/subject/write) asks \strokec5 where to put the write\strokec4  and treats the output string as given. Cluster 3 (token/tokens/prediction/inference) asks \strokec5 how strings are segmented and emitted\strokec4  and treats the weights as given. Cluster 4 (coin/multus/weight/real) names the fragile object \'97 project-coined values \'97 and sits almost unconnected to both, which is the diagnosis: nobody has asked whether a coined value \strokec5 has to be a string at read time at all\strokec4 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The gateway nodes tell you the same thing. `[[prediction]]` and `keyed` both have high betweenness and no edge between them. The missing edge is literally "prediction regime \'d7 keying." That's the proposal.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 The concrete form\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 At write time you know the full target. So:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 Make the edit install a \strokec5 selector\strokec3 , not a \strokec5 sequence\strokec3 .\strokec4  One key at the subject's last token (CORPUS/43 already licenses this \'97 subject-keyed singles transfer 18/18), writing a value vector that a short multi-token-prediction head decodes in one parallel commit, with self-speculative verification as the accept test. The \strokec5 p\strokec4 \uc0\u8319  exponent stops existing because there is no n-step serial chain to compound. Fast SceneScript's own framing: MTP alone degrades, SSD recovers it \'97 the pair is the mechanism, not either half.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This is not AnyEdit (which writes n keys for n tokens \'97 still serial, still 17 CUDA bindings, still a struck anchor). It's not short codes (which shrink n but keep the chain). It changes the \strokec5 shape of the read\strokec4 , which no arm in CORPUS/35\'9643 has varied. Every one of those runs used greedy autoregressive full-sequence exact-match as the measuring instrument \'97 and that instrument is also the failure mode.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 What this predicts, falsifiably\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 If the exponent is decoding-regime-induced rather than value-induced:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa30\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Teacher-forced per-token accuracy (already 60.2% at baseline, 34.0% at later band) should \strokec3 upper-bound\strokec5  parallel-commit accuracy, not equal it \'97 the chain was costing you more than the tokens were.\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 The A7 \uc0\u8596  A1/A2 gap should \strokec3 compress\strokec5 , because single-token values never paid the exponent and multi-token ones stop paying it.\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 The gap should \strokec3 not\strokec5  compress if the real deficit is prior-absence \'97 in which case coined values are fragile wherever they're read from, and cluster 4 is the true blocker, not cluster 3.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 That last branch is the one that matters. It's the same disconfirmation the STRR screen gives you, from the opposite side, for a different cost.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 The honest cost\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This is an inference-path change, not a MEMIT change \'97 which keeps the engine-untouched constraint but moves the deviation to the decoder. A small head trained over your 24 A7 values on CPU-feasible scale is a weekend, not a multi-day CUDA port with a struck reduction anchor. Cheaper than Route A, more informative than another band sweep\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec6 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \'97 which has not moved the number once in three runs.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 What the gateway nodes say about where to put the effort\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 `[[prediction]]` and `keyed` are both high-betweenness and unconnected. `[[teacher]]` sits in a cluster the program has treated as unrelated pedagogy text, but it's also the node attached to `[[prediction]]` via teacher-forcing and distillation \'97 and teacher-forced continuation is already one of your measured diagnostics (60.2 \uc0\u8594  34.0). You have the teacher-forced number and the free-running number side by side in C10e and have been reading the gap as noise. It isn't noise; it's the exposure-bias term. Seer-forcing and HPSE both name it directly: the conventional decoder underperforms a decoder that had access to future/privileged state, and the fix is distillation from the privileged state into the deployed one.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 That reframes the proposal without needing a new head at all:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 C10's residual may be partly an exposure-bias gap, not a write-strength gap.\strokec4  Teacher-forced 60.2% vs parafull 13.9% at the same band is a 46-point chasm between "the write is there when the prefix is correct" and "the write survives its own sampling." No band knob touches that. No per-token edit touches it either \'97 AnyEdit improves what's written, not what happens when the first emitted token drifts and the chain never recovers.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 The one-run test that separates all three hypotheses\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Take the 24 A7 values. Score each under three reads at the same checkpoint:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 | Read | Measures | If high |\
|---|---|---|\
| Teacher-forced full continuation | is the value \strokec5 in\strokec4  W | write succeeded |\
| Free-running, first token constrained to gold | does the chain recover given a correct start | exposure bias is the gap |\
| Free-running, unconstrained (= current parafull) | deployment number | \'97 |\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa30\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 TF high, constrained high, unconstrained low\strokec5  \uc0\u8594  first-token failure dominates (exactly the ACL MoE anecdote: "the first word is incorrect, the remaining part correct"). Fix is first-token-targeted, cheap, no port.\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 TF high, constrained also low\strokec5  \uc0\u8594  the chain itself is unstable; parallel-commit / MTP-style decoding is the right lever, and value-shortening helps for the same reason.\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 TF low\strokec5  \uc0\u8594  the write never landed. Back to AnyEdit, and the port is justified.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Cost: one evaluation pass over existing checkpoints. No new training, no CUDA, no vocabulary change, no engine modification. It partitions the 19\'9631% residual into write-failure, start-failure, and chain-failure \'97 three different fixes that the current single metric cannot distinguish.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 Why this is the move that leaves the graph\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Every arm run so far varies something in cluster 2 (where/how wide/how strong the write) and measures with a fixed instrument from cluster 3 (greedy full-sequence exact match). The instrument has never been a variable. Making it one costs a day and can retire or justify a multi-day port with a struck anchor \'97 and if the first-token branch fires, it retires Any\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec6 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 Edit's rationale too \'97 because a decoder whose first token drifts is not a write-strength problem, and 17 CUDA bindings bought to fix a write that was already there is the most expensive null result the program could purchase.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 The structural claim, stated plainly\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
